WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.443
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level C one.

2
00:00:05.743 --> 00:00:09.246
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.546 --> 00:00:15.391
Unit twelve: Concessive Clauses. Today you will acknowledge a point before presenting a contrast.

4
00:00:15.691 --> 00:00:18.297
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.233 --> 00:00:22.547
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.383 --> 00:00:24.315
Even though.

7
00:00:25.317 --> 00:00:28.721
Even though means a conjunction showing surprising contrast.

8
00:00:29.317 --> 00:00:31.038
Even though it rained, we went.

9
00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:34.012
Your turn. Say: Even though.

10
00:00:36.867 --> 00:00:38.077
Even though.

11
00:00:38.817 --> 00:00:39.864
Much as.

12
00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:43.709
Much as means a formal concession about feelings.

13
00:00:44.317 --> 00:00:46.363
Much as I like him, I disagree.

14
00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:49.730
Your turn. Say: Much as.

15
00:00:52.567 --> 00:00:53.301
Much as.

16
00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:54.865
While.

17
00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:58.395
While means a conjunction showing contrast.

18
00:00:58.983 --> 00:01:00.913
While he is rich, he is unhappy.

19
00:01:01.517 --> 00:01:03.519
Your turn. Say: While.

20
00:01:06.367 --> 00:01:07.507
While.

21
00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:09.495
Granted that.

22
00:01:10.483 --> 00:01:13.168
Granted that means a formal way to accept a point.

23
00:01:13.783 --> 00:01:15.969
Granted that it is costly, it works.

24
00:01:16.583 --> 00:01:19.247
Your turn. Say: Granted that.

25
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:23.240
Granted that.

26
00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:24.996
Concession.

27
00:01:25.983 --> 00:01:28.877
Concession means accepting part of an opposing view.

28
00:01:29.483 --> 00:01:30.810
A fair concession.

29
00:01:31.417 --> 00:01:33.825
Your turn. Say: Concession.

30
00:01:36.667 --> 00:01:37.598
Concession.

31
00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:39.874
Acknowledging a point.

32
00:01:40.883 --> 00:01:44.230
Acknowledging a point means admitting that someone is partly right.

33
00:01:44.817 --> 00:01:46.909
Acknowledging a point builds trust.

34
00:01:47.517 --> 00:01:51.144
Your turn. Say: Acknowledging a point.

35
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.338
Acknowledging a point.

36
00:01:56.083 --> 00:01:58.036
Contrast despite agreement.

37
00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:02.814
Contrast despite agreement means disagreeing after agreeing partly.

38
00:02:03.417 --> 00:02:05.091
Contrast despite agreement.

39
00:02:05.683 --> 00:02:08.730
Your turn. Say: Contrast despite agreement.

40
00:02:11.567 --> 00:02:13.311
Contrast despite agreement.

41
00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:15.457
Formal concession.

42
00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:19.889
Formal concession means a polite admission in a formal text.

43
00:02:20.483 --> 00:02:21.856
A formal concession.

44
00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:25.625
Your turn. Say: Formal concession.

45
00:02:28.467 --> 00:02:29.735
Formal concession.

46
00:02:30.533 --> 00:02:34.196
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:39.697
Visal, how can I disagree politely after admitting that my opponent has a point?

48
00:02:40.783 --> 00:02:51.389
Use a concessive clause: Much as I admire her, I cannot support the plan. It first accepts the opposing feeling, then states your contrary view.

49
00:02:52.483 --> 00:02:54.100
Which conjunctions work?

50
00:02:55.183 --> 00:03:05.687
Although, even though, while, whereas, and much as. Granted that and admittedly are more formal. Note that even though is stronger than although.

51
00:03:06.783 --> 00:03:08.377
Any structural traps?

52
00:03:09.483 --> 00:03:21.029
Do not use but with although: Although it rained, but we went is wrong. Also, much as is followed by a verb of feeling, and the clause can come first or second.

53
00:03:22.167 --> 00:03:23.760
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:24.617 --> 00:03:29.786
Grammar focus: Concession: concessive conjunction plus clause, main clause.

55
00:03:30.086 --> 00:03:31.307
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:32.017 --> 00:03:35.119
Much as I admire her, I cannot support the plan.

57
00:03:36.017 --> 00:03:38.481
Even though it rained, we played outside.

58
00:03:39.383 --> 00:03:42.114
Granted that it is costly, the policy works.

59
00:03:43.067 --> 00:03:48.947
Use although, even though, while, much as or granted that to concede a point. Never add but in the main clause.

60
00:03:49.683 --> 00:03:53.369
Be careful! Don't say: Although tired, but I went.

61
00:03:55.333 --> 00:03:58.215
Say: Although I was tired, I went.

62
00:03:58.515 --> 00:04:00.039
Do not use although and but.

63
00:04:00.833 --> 00:04:03.567
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:04.367 --> 00:04:05.507
Fair but Firm.

65
00:04:05.807 --> 00:04:08.944
Much as I admire her work, I cannot support the plan.

66
00:04:09.933 --> 00:04:12.966
Even though the cost is high, the benefits are clear.

67
00:04:13.967 --> 00:04:17.534
Granted that the method is slow, it is also reliable.

68
00:04:18.533 --> 00:04:21.392
While some people disagree, most accept the result.

69
00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:25.537
Concession shows that a writer has considered other views.

70
00:04:26.633 --> 00:04:30.461
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:31.317 --> 00:04:33.548
blank it rained, we played outside.

72
00:04:33.848 --> 00:04:37.618
A, Even though. B, Because. C, So.

73
00:04:41.167 --> 00:04:43.039
The answer is A: Even though.

74
00:04:44.183 --> 00:04:46.612
Much blank I admire her, I disagree.

75
00:04:46.912 --> 00:04:49.974
A, as. B, so. C, than.

76
00:04:53.533 --> 00:04:55.068
The answer is A: as.

77
00:04:56.217 --> 00:04:57.380
Which is correct?

78
00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:04.410
A, Although tired, but I went.. B, Although I was tired, I went.. C, Although I was tired, but I went..

79
00:05:07.967 --> 00:05:10.791
The answer is B: Although I was tired, I went..

80
00:05:11.950 --> 00:05:14.240
A concession blank part of another view.

81
00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:18.158
A, accepts. B, ignores. C, hides.

82
00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:23.909
The answer is A: accepts.

83
00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:27.502
According to the reading, what is reliable?

84
00:05:27.802 --> 00:05:31.746
A, the method. B, the weather. C, the price.

85
00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:37.218
The answer is A: the method.

86
00:05:38.433 --> 00:05:41.643
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:42.483 --> 00:05:45.063
Write a sentence using 'much as' to concede a point.

88
00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:55.339
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:55.639 --> 00:06:07.744
Much as I enjoy social media, I think it wastes a lot of time. I accept that it connects people, but I still disagree with spending hours on it. Much as shows a polite concession.

90
00:06:08.044 --> 00:06:09.823
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:10.717 --> 00:06:16.922
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and concession: concessive conjunction plus clause, main clause.

92
00:06:17.222 --> 00:06:22.498
Today's value is open-mindedness. Be open to new places, people and ideas.

93
00:06:22.798 --> 00:06:25.485
See you in Unit thirteen. Goodbye!

94
00:06:25.785 --> 00:06:29.134
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

